        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="4" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="21.1" heatingNighttime="21.1" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <windows>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the 3 basement windows to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, although slider windows may be required to maintain adequate egress from the basement in case of a fire.



The windows on the upper floor appear to be still in good shape, and they have a low-E coating, so it is assumed they also have an argon gas fill between the two panes. I am not recommending upgrading the main floor windows at this time..



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the 3 basement windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 102&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has an oil hot water tank (wrapped in insulation) that is co-vented together with the furnace up the chimney It appears to be a 30 USG size tank, that would have a default energy factor of 0.54. As outlined in my furnace upgrade recommendation, I am looking to increase the efficiency of both the furnace and the hot water tank, while eliminating the need for the chimney. I would recommend you consider replacing this tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 229&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.7GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace was originally rated at 82.1% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), and it vents up a 5" chimney, that is co-vented together with the 6" chimney from the hot water tank. I did not see a combustion air supply for the furnace and hot water tank (so they rely on air inside the house for combustion), nor did I see a fresh air supply connected to the return air plenum of the furnace (so fresh air into the house only enters through any small holes in the building envelope).



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace will draw its' combustion air directly from outside. Later in this report, you will see an upgrade recommendation for the hot water tank - and upgrading both appliances will allow you to completely eliminate the current chimney &amp; ensure outside air is used for combustion ........... while significantly increasing the efficiency of both appliances.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,258&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;27.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front SE facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 850&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 in a diesel electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Using Biomass - Wood Stove &lt;/b&gt;



You currently have a wood stove on the main floor, that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe. You have created a heat distribution system (with an inline fan) to help push some of that wood stove heat into the basement. Our software can model the estimated savings if you use the wood stove to offset 100% of the heating load of the main floor, however cannot estimate how much the offset would be for the basement ..... using the custom design heat transfer fan system that you have installed.



So ............ I will estimate what I can:



1. If you use your wood stove to offset 100% of the heating load requirement of the upper floor (compared to the current 82.1% AFUE oil furnace), the estimated annual savings are $1,570. This assumes a $100 per cord cost for self-harvested wood, compared to the cost of oil (outlined in the chart a little further down in this section).



2. If you use your wood stove to offset 100% of the heating load requirement of the upper floor (compared to the new 95% AFUE propane furnace), the estimated annual savings are $2,217. These savings include the $1,258 of savings associated with upgrading the current oil furnace to the high efficiency propane furnace - ie: there would be an additional $959 of savings for heating the upstairs with wood. This assumes a $100 per cord cost for self-harvested wood, compared to the cost of propane (outlined in the chart a little further down in this section).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has very little ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood fan does not vent to the outside.

2. The 2 bathroom fans have been terminated in the attic and covered with insulation.

3. There is no combustion air supply pipe for the current furnace and hot water tank, so they both draw upon interior house air for combustion.

4. There is no fresh air supply duct connected to the return air plenum of the furnace.

5. The woodstove does have a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe connected, so the woodstove does not draw combustion air from inside the house.



I would recommend you consider upgrading both bathroom fans to ENERGY STAR rated units connected to a humidistat, or a timer switch, and ensure these bathroom fans are once again vented outside of the attic space.



If you upgrade the furnace and hot water (as per the options modeled within this report), the new units will be high efficiency, direct vent units ...... drawing their combustion air from outside and eliminating the need for the current chimney.



Your heating contractor should review the opportunity to install a fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace. This fresh air supply would include a manual damper so that you can adjust the amount of fresh air entering the house seasonally.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood for does not vent outside, the 2 bathroom fans have been terminated in the attic &amp; covered with insulation, and the dryer which does vent to the outside, indicated a pressure drop of 3.7 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace and hot water tank co-vent up a chimney. The current woodstove has a dedicated combustion air supply, however can still be susceptible to depressurization (drawing smoke into the house) each time the door is opened to add wood.



You can expect the amount of depressurization to increase if the furnace &amp; hot water appliances are upgraded to high efficiency direct-vent units (eliminating the chimney) and the 2 bathroom fans are once again connected to vent to the exterior of the house.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



